export const title = "TCP Modbus (WAVESHARE)";
export const html = "<h1>TCP Modbus (WAVESHARE)</h1>\n      <hr class=\"rule\" />\n\n<h2>1. Introduction</h2>\n<h3>1.1 Prerequisites</h3>\n<ul class=\"steps\">\n<li>AI Bridge firmware version 103719 or later.</li>\n<li>TCP Modbus relay box &mdash; recommended alarm box models: <a href=\"https://www.waveshare.com/modbus-poe-eth-relay.htm\">Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay</a>, <a href=\"https://www.waveshare.com/product/modbus-poe-eth-relayb.htm.htm\">Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay (B)</a>, <a href=\"https://www.waveshare.com/product/modbus-poe-eth-relay30ch.htm.htm\">Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay 30CH</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">1.2 Understanding the Integration Architecture</h3>\n<p>There are two ways to integrate AI Bridge with the alarm box (Modbus POE ETH Relay).</p>\n<p><strong>Case 1: connect the relay directly to AI Bridge's LAN2 port</strong> &ndash; connect the Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay (\"the relay\") to AI Bridge's LAN2 port. AI Bridge's LAN2 needs an assigned IP address, and the relay needs to be powered &mdash; either through a PoE injector or by supplying external power to the relay. If you assign AI Bridge's LAN2 IP address as 192.168.1.xxx, the relay's default IP of 192.168.1.200 is used automatically, so there's no need to set an IP on the relay separately.</p>\n<p><strong>Case 2: connect via LAN1 through a switch (not recommended)</strong> &ndash; here the relay connects to the LAN1 port. This setup requires configuring the switch, and if the switch doesn't support Power over Ethernet (PoE), you'll need to supply additional power to the relay and change its IP address. However, because Modbus TCP has no authentication mechanism, unauthorized network access is a security risk, so this method isn't recommended.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Configuration</h2>\n<p>To integrate AI Bridge with the Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay, each device needs to be configured correctly. The steps below assume Case 1. If you prefer Case 2, see section 3.1 for additional setup.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"margin-top:24px;\">2.1 AI Bridge</h3>\n<p>To use AI Bridge with Modbus hardware:</p>\n<h4 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">2.1.1 Network Settings</h4>\n<p>Since Case 1 is recommended, you need to configure Ethernet2. Here's how.</p>\n<p>Once logged into the AI Bridge web page, click <strong>System Settings &rsaquo; Network Settings</strong> from the left menu.</p>\n<p>Click <strong>Ethernet2</strong>, turn off DHCP, and set an IP address and subnet mask.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:460px;\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:12px;\">\n    <span class=\"btn small\">Ethernet1</span><span class=\"btn small primary\">Ethernet2</span><span class=\"btn small\">Metrics</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"form-grid\" style=\"grid-template-columns:120px 1fr;font-size:12.5px;\">\n    <span>DHCP</span><span style=\"background:#e7e9f0;border-radius:10px;width:34px;height:18px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"><span style=\"position:absolute;left:2px;top:2px;width:14px;height:14px;background:#fff;border-radius:50%;\"></span></span>\n    <span>IP Address</span><input value=\"192.168.1.201\" disabled>\n    <span>Subnet Mask</span><input value=\"255.255.255.0\" disabled>\n    <span>Gateway Address</span><input value=\"0.0.0.0\" disabled>\n    <span>DNS 1</span><input value=\"0.0.0.0\" disabled>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Ethernet2 &mdash; disable DHCP and set a static IP</figcaption>\n</div>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Note: if only the relay device is connected to eth2, don't configure a gateway address or DNS 1.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h4 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">2.1.2 Modbus Relay Setup</h4>\n<p>Log into the web page and click <strong>App Settings &rsaquo; System Event Settings &rsaquo; Modbus</strong> from the left menu.</p>\n<p>Select <strong>TCP</strong> as the transport, and enter the host and port number. For the Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay, the device address is fixed at 0x01.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:360px;\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n  <div class=\"form-grid\" style=\"grid-template-columns:100px 1fr;font-size:12.5px;\">\n    <span>Transport</span><select disabled><option>TCP</option></select>\n    <span>Host</span><input value=\"192.168.1.200\" disabled>\n    <span>Port</span><input value=\"4196\" disabled>\n    <span>Device Address</span><span>0x <input value=\"01\" disabled style=\"width:50px;\"></span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Modbus device settings</figcaption>\n</div>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Note: the device's default IP is 192.168.1.200, and the port is 4196.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Enter the following configuration values: for the Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay, the function is fixed at 0x05, the start address is 0x0000, ON is 0xff00, and OFF is 0x0000.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\">\n<div class=\"app-panel\">\n<table class=\"app-table\">\n<tr><th>Id</th><th>Name</th><th>Function</th><th>Start Address</th><th>ON</th><th>OFF</th><th>Test</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Modbus Relay 1</td><td>0x 05</td><td>0x 0000</td><td>0x ff00</td><td>0x 0000</td><td>ON &nbsp; OFF</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Modbus Relay 2</td><td>0x 05</td><td>0x 0001</td><td>0x ff00</td><td>0x 0000</td><td>ON &nbsp; OFF</td></tr>\n</table>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Modbus Relay (20)</figcaption>\n</div>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 4px;\">Tip: click the Refresh button to check the current relay state of the connected device.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Tip: name each relay and click the ON/OFF buttons to test it.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h4 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">2.1.3 Modbus Relay Action Settings</h4>\n<p>To add a relay device registered on AI Bridge to an action setting:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>After configuring an AI app or a system event, select <strong>Add Action Setting</strong>.</li>\n<li>Select <strong>Modbus Relay</strong> as the action type.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Add Modbus Relay Action</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Index</strong> refers to which relay device (configured in 2.1.2) to control. Choose the output type you want, then click <strong>Test</strong> to confirm normal operation. Once configured, click <strong>Apply</strong> to save.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>OK</strong> to confirm.</li>\n<li>Check the checkbox, then click <strong>Apply</strong>.</li>\n<li>Once configuration is complete, click <strong>Save</strong> to apply the changes.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>3. FAQ</h2>\n<h3>3.1 Configuring the Waveshare Modbus POE ETH Relay</h3>\n<p>To find and configure the device, download the installer below. This tool is a zip file containing a Windows executable.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/141zWJjZfnPz7vCLxpu0ptRyppbtMCmHs/view?usp=drive_link\">VirCom tool download link</a></p>\n\n<h4 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">3.1.1 Discovering the Device</h4>\n<p>If your Windows PC is on the same network as the relay device, download and unzip the VirCom tool and run it. Click the <strong>Device</strong> button.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"max-width:520px;\">\n<div class=\"app-window\">\n  <div class=\"app-titlebar\"><span>Virtual Serial &amp; Device Management &ndash; VirCom</span><span>&ndash; &#9633; &times;</span></div>\n  <div style=\"padding:14px 18px;font-size:12px;\">\n    <div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n      <span class=\"btn small\">Start</span><span class=\"btn small\">Stop</span><span class=\"btn small primary\">Device</span><span class=\"btn small\">Serial</span><span class=\"btn small\">About</span>\n    </div>\n    <table class=\"app-table\"><tr><th>In...</th><th>Status</th><th>Com Name</th><th>COM Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Device IP</th></tr></table>\n    <div style=\"background:#0e1420;color:#7fbf95;font-size:10.5px;padding:8px;border-radius:4px;margin-top:8px;font-family:monospace;\">\n      [Create ok!]<br>[Create ok!]<br>[Listen at port 4196 OK.]\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<figcaption>VirCom &mdash; Device search</figcaption>\n</div>\n<p>Devices on the same network are detected. If no device is found, check the network connection and the device's power state.</p>\n\n<h4 style=\"margin-top:16px;\">3.1.2 Configuration</h4>\n<p>Connect to the device you found in 3.1.1.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Note: the device has no default password, so you can log in with any value or leave it blank.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Set the device's IP and port, and configure the working mode as TCP Server.</p>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Note: the device's default IP is 192.168.1.200, and the port is 4196.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"figure\" style=\"border-left:3px solid var(--green);background:#fafbfc;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Tip: when setting up multiple devices, configure them one at a time. Since every device starts with the same default IP address, configuring several devices simultaneously can cause conflicts, and configuring one device can affect another. To avoid this, finish configuring one device fully before moving on to the next.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Revision History</h2>\n<ul class=\"steps\"><li>v1.00 &ndash; initial document</li><li>v1.10 &ndash; added recommended alarm box models</li><li>v1.20 &ndash; added detailed explanations</li></ul>";
